<p>The product cache in the <ArticleRef slug="">introduction</ArticleRef> stores each page with one command: <code>SET product:42 … EX 60</code>. The <code>EX 60</code> gives the key a time to live (TTL) of 60 seconds, which bounds how long a customer can see an old price. Why 60 and not 5, or 3,600? The number decides how stale the page may be and how often the database is asked for it again, and so far nothing chose it except habit.</p>
<p>A cache loses keys in two ways. <em>Expiry</em> removes a key because its time is up. <em>Eviction</em> removes a key because Redis needs the memory for another one. You control the first with TTLs and the second with a memory limit and an eviction policy. Out of the box, Redis has no memory limit at all, and once you set one it refuses new writes rather than discarding anything. Neither default suits a cache.</p>
<p>By the end you will be able to choose a TTL, a memory limit and an eviction policy, and tell from Redis's counters which kind of removal is costing you hits.</p>

<section id="ttl">
<h2>What a TTL buys and what it costs</h2>
<p>The introduction showed that a TTL bounds staleness: if nothing deletes the key when the data changes, Redis serves its copy until the TTL runs out. Here we assume nothing deletes it. That is also the situation whenever such a delete goes missing. <ArticleRef slug="invalidation">Cache invalidation and consistency</ArticleRef> is about deleting keys on change.</p>
<p>Figure 1 follows a single key. It is read about once a second. Its value in the <Term kind="database">database</Term> changes about once every 30 seconds, shown as a <Term kind="write" mark>write</Term> on the top row. A <Term kind="miss" mark>miss</Term> reads the database and stores a copy in <Term kind="redis">Redis</Term>, drawn as a bar on the middle row, until the copy reaches its <Term kind="expiry" mark>expiry</Term>. Reads in between are <Term kind="hit" mark>hits</Term>, unless the database has changed since the copy was made, in which case the hit returns a <Term kind="stale" mark>stale</Term> value.</p>
<TtlTimeline number={1} />
<p>Every bar starts with a miss, and triangles appear only after a square, late in a bar. Drag the TTL up to 30 seconds and let it run for a minute. The bars get longer, so misses become rare, but more of each bar comes after a change, so more of the hits are stale. Drag it down to 3 seconds and the opposite happens. Moving the slider drops the cached copy, so the next read stores one with the new TTL, and each ‘so far’ readout counts from that read, beside the long-run value it is heading for. Changes are rare, so the stale readout jumps whenever one lands and takes a while to settle.</p>
<p>The trade has a simple shape, and it is worth deriving because it tells you which knob matters for which key. Say the key is read at random at a rate of <var>r</var> reads a second and the TTL is <var>T</var> seconds. Each cycle starts with one miss, and the copy then lives for <var>T</var> seconds, during which about <var>rT</var> reads arrive and hit. So:</p>
<div class="ee-math">hit ratio = rT / (1 + rT)<br />database reads a second = 1 / (T + 1/r)</div>
<p>Now say the database value changes at random at a rate of <var>λ</var> changes a second. If changes arrive independently of each other (a Poisson process), the chance that none arrives in <var>a</var> seconds is <span class="nowrap">e<sup>−λa</sup></span>. A hit that comes <var>a</var> seconds after the copy was made is stale if at least one change did arrive, which has probability <span class="nowrap">1 − e<sup>−λa</sup></span>. Hits are spread evenly over the copy's life, so the average over <var>a</var> from 0 to <var>T</var> is the share of hits that are stale:</p>
<div class="ee-math">stale share of hits = 1 − (1 − e<sup>−λT</sup>) / (λT) ≈ λT / 2 when λT is small</div>
<p>In Figure 1, <var>r</var> is 1 and <var>λ</var> is 1/30. A 10-second TTL gives 10/11, about 91% hits, and about 15% of hits stale. The worst case is easier still: a copy can be at most <var>T</var> seconds out of date, provided it was correct when it was stored. (<ArticleRef slug="invalidation">Cache invalidation and consistency</ArticleRef> shows how a racing write can store a copy that was already wrong.)</p>
<p>Look at which variables appear where. The stale share depends on how often the value changes and on the TTL, not on how often the key is read. The hit ratio depends on how often the key is read and on the TTL, not on how often the value changes. Figure 2 draws both for a product page whose price changes about once an hour. Switch between the read rates.</p>
<TtlTradeoff number={2} />
<p>The stale curve does not move. Every key with the same change rate pays the same staleness for the same TTL, while the hit ratio it gets in return depends entirely on its popularity. A page read once a second passes 90% hits with a 9-second TTL, when about one hit in 800 is stale. A page read once a minute needs 9 minutes for the same hit ratio, and by then about 7% of its hits are stale. A page read once an hour cannot reach a good hit ratio with any TTL you would accept.</p>
<p>That gives an order for choosing a TTL. First decide how long a wrong value may be shown: a product description can be an hour out of date, a price perhaps a minute, a stock count shown at checkout not at all. That is the longest TTL you may use. Then look at what it buys. Popular keys get nearly all their hits within a few seconds of TTL, so a long TTL adds little for them except staleness. Rarely read keys need TTLs longer than the staleness you can accept, so for them a TTL cannot buy a high hit ratio. When the tolerable staleness is near zero, a TTL is the wrong tool and you need invalidation on write, with a TTL kept as a safety net for invalidations that go missing.</p>
<p>A TTL also frees memory. Many keys are written once and never read again: search results, a session that was abandoned, the page of a product nobody else looks at. Their TTL is the only thing that will ever remove them unless memory runs out. Facebook's memcache deployment added a structure that removes short-lived keys as soon as they expire; when it then gave one heavily used family of keys a short expiration time, that family's share of the memory pool fell from 6% to 0.3% without affecting the hit rate. That raises a question about Redis: when a TTL runs out, does the memory come back straight away?</p>
</section>

<section id="how-redis-expires">
<h2>How Redis expires keys</h2>
<p>Redis stores a key's TTL as an absolute time, a Unix timestamp in milliseconds, so the clock keeps running even while the server is stopped. From the outside, expiry is precise: a key I set with <code>PX</code> 200 (a TTL of 200 milliseconds) on Redis 8.10.3 was last readable between 200.1 and 200.7 ms after the <code>SET</code>, and the documentation gives the accuracy as 0 to 1 millisecond.</p>
<p>What Redis does not have is a timer per key. Keeping millions of timers would cost memory and time. Instead it removes expired keys in two ways. <em>Passive expiry</em>: when a command touches a key, Redis checks its TTL first, and if the time has passed it deletes the key there and then and answers as if it had never existed. <em>Active expiry</em>: a background cycle looks through keys that have a TTL and deletes the expired ones it finds, so that keys nobody touches again are still reclaimed.</p>
<p>Between the moment a TTL passes and the moment one of those two things happens, the key is in an odd state. No read can see it, but it still occupies memory. Figure 3 shows keys like search results: written once, read a few times while they are fresh, then never again. Switch the background cycle off and watch what accumulates.</p>
<ExpiredHeld number={3} />
<p>With the cycle off, only a read can remove an expired key, and reads only ask for recent keys, so the old ones pile up. Nothing a client can see has changed: every read still gets the right answer. Only the memory figures show it.</p>
<p>A real server shows the same. With the cycle switched off (<code>DEBUG SET-ACTIVE-EXPIRE 0</code>, a debugging command that is disabled by default and has no place in production), I wrote 200,000 keys with 100-byte values and <code>PX 500</code>. Two and a half seconds later every one had expired, yet <code>DBSIZE</code> still reported 200,000 keys and <code>used_memory</code> was 39.1 MB, against 0.9 MB when empty. When I switched the cycle back on, every key was gone within half a second.</p>
<p>That half second is the best case. Ten times a second (the default <code>hz</code> of 10), the cycle walks on through the keys that have a TTL, 20 at a time, deleting the expired ones it passes. It moves on to the next 20 only while more than 10% of the last 20 had expired, and it stops after 25 ms. In the test above every batch was full of expired keys, so the cycle never stopped early.</p>
<p>In a real cache, expired keys are usually a small share of the keys with a TTL. Most batches then find one or two, the cycle stops, and it examines only about 200 keys a second. A burst of expiries is cleared quickly only on a busy server, where Redis also runs short extra cycles between client requests while it estimates that 10% or more have expired. When I let a fifth of the keys with a TTL expire together, a busy server cleared nearly all of them within 20 seconds; an idle one still held most of them 40 seconds later.</p>
<p>The configuration file states the aim plainly: to avoid having more than ten percent of expired keys still in memory. Treat that as the steady state of a busy server, not a limit. After a burst of expiries, or on a quiet server, a fifth or more of the keys with a TTL can be expired and still held for minutes. And a single expired key can wait for the cycle's full pass over the keys with a TTL, which at the default <code>hz</code> takes roughly the number of such keys divided by 200, in seconds: over an hour for a million keys. The setting <code>active-expire-effort</code>, from 1 (the default) to 10, makes the cycle examine more keys per batch, tolerate a lower share and spend more CPU.</p>
<p>Three consequences are worth knowing.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Memory and key counts include the dead.</strong> <code>DBSIZE</code>, the <code>keys=</code> figure in <code>INFO keyspace</code>, and <code>used_memory</code> all count keys that have expired but have not yet been found. Size memory for them: a tenth of the keys with a TTL, more after a burst.</li>
<li><strong>Expiry notifications can arrive much later than the TTL.</strong> If you subscribe to Redis's keyspace notifications, the <code>expired</code> event is sent when the key is deleted, which for a key nobody reads can be minutes or hours after its TTL. Do not build a timer on it.</li>
<li><strong>Replicas wait for the primary.</strong> A replica does not delete expired keys itself; it waits for the primary to send a delete, though it hides logically expired keys from reads in the meantime. <ArticleRef slug="replication-and-persistence">Redis replication, persistence and failover</ArticleRef> covers replication.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Setting, reading and losing a TTL</h3>
<p>Most of the time you set a TTL in the same command that writes the value, so there is no moment when the key exists without one: <code>SET key value EX seconds</code> (or <code>PX</code> for milliseconds, or <code>EXAT</code> and <code>PXAT</code> for an absolute Unix time). The other commands change or read a TTL on an existing key. I checked each behaviour below on Redis 8.10.3.</p>
<div class="ee-table-wrap"><table class="ee-table">
<thead><tr><th>Command</th><th>What it does to the TTL</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><code>EXPIRE key 600</code>, <code>PEXPIRE</code>, <code>EXPIREAT</code></td><td>Sets it. A time in the past deletes the key at once. Since Redis 7.0, <code>NX</code> sets it only if the key has none, <code>XX</code> only if it has one, <code>GT</code> only if the new TTL is longer and <code>LT</code> only if shorter. A key without a TTL counts as having an infinite one, so <code>GT</code> never adds a TTL to it.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>TTL key</code>, <code>PTTL key</code></td><td>Reads the remaining time in seconds or milliseconds: <code>-1</code> means no TTL, <code>-2</code> means no such key.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>SET key value</code> (no <code>EX</code>)</td><td><strong>Clears it.</strong> The key now never expires.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>SET key value KEEPTTL</code> (6.0)</td><td>Keeps the existing TTL.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>GETEX key EX 60</code> (6.2)</td><td>Reads the value and sets a new TTL in one command; <code>GETEX key PERSIST</code> removes it.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>PERSIST key</code></td><td>Removes it.</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p>The third row is the trap that <ArticleRef slug="caching-patterns">Caching patterns</ArticleRef> warned about for write-through: any plain <code>SET</code> turns a cached key into a permanent one, and nothing fails. Under some eviction policies, as we will see shortly, such a key can never be evicted either. Restate the TTL on every write, or use <code>KEEPTTL</code> when keeping the old one is what you mean.</p>
<p>Redis 7.4 added TTLs on individual fields of a hash (<code>HEXPIRE</code>), which <ArticleRef slug="keys-and-values">Cache keys and cached values</ArticleRef> covers. A hash whose fields have TTLs but which has no TTL of its own counts as a key without a TTL for the purposes of eviction.</p>
</section>

<section id="jitter">
<h2>Synchronised expiry and TTL jitter</h2>
<p>A TTL is counted from the moment a key is written. Keys written at the same moment with the same TTL therefore expire at the same moment. That happens more often than it sounds: after a deploy that empties the cache, after a job that warms the cache by loading every product, or after a burst of traffic fills thousands of keys within a minute.</p>
<p>Figure 4 fills 10,000 keys at once with a 10-minute TTL. Each key is read often enough that it is refilled the moment it expires, so every expiry costs one database read. Start with no jitter.</p>
<JitterWaves number={4} />
<p>With no jitter, every key expires in the same instant, refills in the same instant, and expires together again ten minutes later, forever. The database sees nothing, then 10,000 reads at once, then nothing. Now move the slider to ±10%, which draws each key's TTL at random from 9 to 11 minutes.</p>
<p>The arithmetic is short. If <var>N</var> keys get TTLs spread evenly across <span class="nowrap">T(1 − j)</span> to <span class="nowrap">T(1 + j)</span>, their expiries spread over <span class="nowrap">2jT</span> seconds, so the refill rate during the first wave is about:</p>
<div class="ee-math">N / (2jT) = 10,000 / (2 × 0.1 × 600) ≈ 83 refills a second</div>
<p>That is still five times the long-run average of <span class="nowrap">N / T ≈ 16.7</span> a second, but it arrives over two minutes instead of in one instant. Notice the later waves. Each refill draws a new random TTL, and the sum of several random TTLs varies more than one does, so each wave is wider and lower than the one before. Jitter does not just soften the first wave; it keeps pulling the keys out of step for as long as they are refilled.</p>
<p>The database is not the only thing that feels a synchronised expiry. Redis itself has to delete the keys, on the same thread that answers clients. I wrote 1,000,000 keys with 100-byte values, all set to expire in the same millisecond, and sent <code>PING</code> from a second connection about every millisecond. Before the expiry, the slowest reply in a half second was usually about 0.25 ms. Once the keys expired, the active cycle deleted them over about 1.5 seconds, and in every half second of that time the slowest <code>PING</code> took 26 ms: the cycle's 25 ms slice at <code>hz</code> 10, during which nothing else ran. (With many clients connected, Redis raises <code>hz</code> on its own, which makes each slice shorter but keeps the share of time the same.) With the same million keys spread evenly over 30 seconds, no half second had a reply slower than 13 ms, and most stayed under 8 ms. The spread run spent more CPU on expiry in total (1.26 seconds against 0.6), so jitter does not make expiry cheaper. It removes the pause that clients see.</p>
<p>Adding jitter takes one line where you set the TTL, for example <code>EX</code> round(600 × (0.9 + 0.2 × random())), since <code>EX</code> takes whole seconds. It costs nothing in staleness as long as the longest jittered TTL is within your tolerance. One hot key expiring under heavy traffic is a different problem, a stampede, because many requests miss on the same key at once; jitter cannot help with that, and <ArticleRef slug="stampedes">Cache stampedes and the thundering herd</ArticleRef> covers it.</p>
</section>

<section id="maxmemory">
<h2>When memory runs out</h2>
<p>Expiry frees memory only when a TTL runs out. If keys arrive faster than they expire, or have no TTL, the cache grows until something stops it. In Redis that something is <code>maxmemory</code>, and its default on 64-bit systems is 0, meaning no limit: Redis keeps allocating until the operating system runs out of memory or kills the process. A cache should always have a limit, set in the configuration file or at runtime with <code>CONFIG SET maxmemory 2gb</code>.</p>
<p>The limit alone is not enough, because of the second default. The <em>eviction policy</em>, <code>maxmemory-policy</code>, decides what Redis does when a command needs memory above the limit, and it defaults to <code>noeviction</code>: remove nothing and refuse the command. I set <code>maxmemory 20mb</code> and wrote 200,000 keys with 100-byte values. Redis stored 126,729 of them and answered the other 73,271 with this error:</p>
<pre><code>OOM command not allowed when used memory &gt; 'maxmemory'.</code></pre>
<p>Reads still worked, as did <code>DEL</code>. Redis refused every command that may need more memory, including <code>INCR</code> on a new key and a plain <code>SET</code> that overwrites an existing one. For a cache that uses cache-aside, every miss now fails to store its value, so every later read of that key misses too.</p>
<p>Redis checks memory before it runs each command, not after, so a single large command can go well over the limit. I filled a Redis that had <code>maxmemory 6mb</code> and an evicting policy with 3,000 keys of 1 KB, then stored one 8 MB value. The <code>SET</code> succeeded. On the next command Redis evicted 2,367 of the small keys and then the 8 MB value itself, leaving 633 keys. One oversized value emptied most of the cache and kept nothing. Freeing a big value also stalls every client, because Redis 8.10 frees evicted and expired values on its main thread by default; <ArticleRef slug="how-redis-works" hash="freeing">How Redis works</ArticleRef> measures that pause and shows the settings that move the work to a background thread.</p>
<p>The memory compared with the limit is Redis's own <code>used_memory</code>, excluding the buffers that feed replicas and the append-only file, so leave room between <code>maxmemory</code> and the machine's memory (<ArticleRef slug="operating-a-cache">Operating a cache</ArticleRef> covers sizing).</p>

<h3>What each policy may remove</h3>
<p>Every eviction policy answers two questions: which keys may be removed, and in what order. Figure 5 sends the same writes into a full Redis under three policies. Some keys carry a TTL, as cache keys should. Others do not: perhaps sessions or counters that share the instance, or cache keys written by code that forgot the TTL. Try each policy and let each run for a minute of simulated time.</p>
<FullRedis number={5} />
<p><code>noeviction</code> refuses the first write after memory fills, and every write after that. <code>volatile-lru</code> removes only keys that have a TTL, choosing the least recently used among them. Writes succeed, but keys without a TTL accumulate, because nothing ever removes them; once they fill memory, the policy has nothing left to evict and refuses writes just as <code>noeviction</code> does. <code>allkeys-lru</code> never refuses a write, and it evicts keys without a TTL as readily as any other. A real server with no TTLs at all did the same under <code>volatile-lru</code>: it refused writes and evicted nothing.</p>
<p>This matters more than it seems, because managed services choose differently from Redis. Amazon ElastiCache's default parameter group sets <code>maxmemory-policy</code> to <code>volatile-lru</code>, and its serverless caches fix it there. On such a service, a cache key without a TTL can never be evicted, so every cache key needs one.</p>
<p>The figure also shows why mixing a cache with data that must not disappear is awkward. Under <code>allkeys-lru</code> the cache can evict your sessions; under <code>volatile-lru</code> the sessions can crowd out the cache. The Redis documentation recommends running two instances, one for each.</p>

<h3>The ten policies</h3>
<p>Redis 8.10 has ten policies. The <code>allkeys</code> family may evict any key; the <code>volatile</code> family only keys that have a TTL, and behaves like <code>noeviction</code> when there are none.</p>
<div class="ee-table-wrap"><table class="ee-table">
<thead><tr><th>Policy</th><th>Evicts</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><code>noeviction</code> (default)</td><td>Nothing; commands that need memory fail.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>allkeys-lru</code>, <code>volatile-lru</code></td><td>The least recently used (LRU) key.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>allkeys-lfu</code>, <code>volatile-lfu</code> (4.0)</td><td>The least frequently used (LFU) key.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>allkeys-random</code>, <code>volatile-random</code></td><td>A random key.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>volatile-ttl</code></td><td>The key with the shortest remaining TTL.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>allkeys-lrm</code>, <code>volatile-lrm</code> (8.6)</td><td>The least recently <em>modified</em> key: reads do not count, only writes.</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p>For a Redis used only as a cache, the choice is between <code>allkeys-lru</code>, which the documentation calls a good default, and <code>allkeys-lfu</code>. The others suit narrower cases: <code>allkeys-random</code> access spread evenly across keys, <code>volatile-ttl</code> code that ranks keys by giving short TTLs to the ones it cares about least. The <code>lrm</code> policies, new in Redis 8.6 and absent from Valkey, drop keys that are only read, which is rarely what a cache wants: despite the documentation's framing of them for read-heavy workloads, a key that is read constantly but never rewritten is the first to go. Choosing between LRU and LFU needs a closer look at how Redis implements them, because neither is what its name suggests.</p>
</section>

<section id="approximated-lru">
<h2>Approximated LRU</h2>
<p>An exact least-recently-used cache keeps every key in a list ordered by last use: each read moves its key to the front, and eviction takes the key at the back. That costs two pointers per key and a list update on every read. Redis avoids both. Each key carries a 24-bit field holding the time of its last access, in whole seconds, and nothing else.</p>
<p>To evict, Redis does not search all the keys for the oldest. It picks <code>maxmemory-samples</code> keys at random (5 by default) and works out how long each has been idle. It keeps a <em>pool</em> of the 16 most idle candidates seen so far, which survives from one eviction to the next: each sampled key goes into the pool if there is room, or if it is idler than the least idle candidate there, which then drops out. Redis evicts the most idle candidate in the pool, skipping any that have already been deleted.</p>
<p>How good is that choice? One measure is how often the evicted key comes from the oldest tenth of keys, where an exact LRU would always find it. Sampling alone, without the pool, manages that unless all <var>k</var> samples missed the oldest tenth:</p>
<div class="ee-math">P(evicted key is in the oldest tenth) = 1 − 0.9<sup>k</sup></div>
<p>That is 10% with one sample, 41% with five and 65% with ten. One sample is random eviction; more samples sharpen the choice, with diminishing returns. Figure 6 runs the whole algorithm, pool included, on 100 keys. Each second it needs room for one new key, and its readout uses the same measure.</p>
<Sampling number={6} />
<p>Watch the outlined keys: they are what Redis looked at this time. The cross marks the key it evicted, which often was not one of them but a candidate left in the pool from earlier. Set the slider to 1 and evictions land anywhere. At 5 about half come from the oldest tenth, a little better than the formula, because the pool remembers good candidates. Above 10 the gain is small.</p>
<p>Missing the very oldest key matters only if it costs hits, and on skewed traffic it hardly does. In this article's model of Redis's eviction, run on the workload of the next section at a steady 5,000 requests a second, 5 samples came within 0.1 points of an exact LRU's hit ratio at the smallest cache size (about 15 requests a minute for each cached key), and 10 or 64 samples gained less than that. At benchmark speed, 64 samples gained next to nothing in the model either. Leave <code>maxmemory-samples</code> at 5.</p>
</section>

<section id="approximated-lfu">
<h2>Approximated LFU: a logarithmic counter with decay</h2>
<p>Least frequently used needs a count of accesses for every key, and the count has to fit in the same 24 bits. Redis splits them: 16 bits hold a time in minutes, and 8 bits hold a counter, so the counter can only go up to 255. A key read a million times needs a counter that counts a million in 255 steps.</p>
<p>The trick is to count in probabilities. A new key starts at 5, so that it survives long enough to be read again. On each access, Redis raises a counter <var>c</var> by one only with probability</p>
<div class="ee-math">p = 1 / ((c − 5) × F + 1)</div>
<p>where <var>F</var> is <code>lfu-log-factor</code>, 10 by default. At 5 every access counts. At 15 only one access in 101 does. This is a <em>Morris counter</em>, named after Robert Morris, who described it in 1978. The expected number of accesses to climb one step from <var>c</var> is <span class="nowrap">(c − 5) × F + 1</span>, so reaching <var>c</var> from 5 takes, on average, the sum of those steps:</p>
<div class="ee-math">accesses to reach c ≈ (c − 5) + F × (c − 5)(c − 6) / 2</div>
<p>With the default factor, that is about 105 accesses to reach 10, about 10,000 to reach 50, and about 311,000 to reach the ceiling of 255. Figure 7 plots the counter against accesses on a scale where each step is ten times more, with values measured on a real server.</p>
<LfuCounter number={7} />
<p>The counter rises quickly through the first few hundred accesses and then slows to a crawl. That is the resolution eviction needs: it separates a key read twice from one read two thousand times, and does not care whether a key was read half a million or five million times, because neither will be evicted. Raise the factor to tell apart keys with millions of reads; lower it if your keys are read only dozens of times each.</p>
<p>A count that only grows would keep yesterday's popular keys forever. The 16-bit time handles that. When Redis reads a key's counter, to update it or to compare it for eviction, it first subtracts one for every <code>lfu-decay-time</code> minutes (default 1) since the key was last accessed. A key I restored with its counter at 100 read 99 a minute later, 98 after two and 97 after three. Most keys' counters sit not far above the starting 5, so a few minutes without a read takes them below it, and they are then worse candidates to keep than a key that was just added. The next section shows how much that matters.</p>
<p>Eviction under LFU works like LRU: sample, keep a pool of 16, evict the candidate with the lowest decayed counter. <code>OBJECT FREQ key</code> shows a key's counter under an LFU policy, and <code>OBJECT IDLETIME key</code> its idle time under the others.</p>
</section>

<section id="lru-or-lfu">
<h2>LRU or LFU: what the hit ratio says</h2>
<p>Which approximation hits more depends on the requests, and also on how fast they arrive. The workload here is cache-aside reads over 4,000,000 keys whose popularity follows a Zipf-like law with exponent 0.8, within the range Breslau and colleagues measured on web proxy traces (0.64 to 0.83). I first ran it on Redis 8.10.3 the way benchmarks usually run: as fast as one client could send, about 80,000 requests a second. Figure 8 shows those measurements as circles, beside an oracle that always holds the most popular keys, the best any cache of that size could do.</p>
<SizeCurve number={8} />
<p>At the benchmark's pace the hit ratio climbs by roughly 5 to 20 points at each step of the axis, and even the oracle misses most requests at 0.5% of the keys. <code>allkeys-lfu</code> beats <code>allkeys-lru</code> by 10 points at the smallest size, and <code>allkeys-lru</code> is barely better than random.</p>
<p>Now switch to 5,000 requests a second, which only the model shows at this size. The LRU line rises a little, and the LFU line falls: its lead at the smallest size halves, and at the largest it becomes a loss. Both policies keep time on the wall clock, LRU in seconds and LFU in minutes, so what matters is how often each cached key is requested. In the benchmark, the smallest cache saw 240 requests a minute for each key it held. At 5,000 a second, the largest sees one every four minutes.</p>
<p><strong>LRU's clock ticks once a second.</strong> In the benchmark, the smallest cache replaced all its keys in about a third of a second, so most keys Redis compared had the same idle time and it chose among ties at random. That cost 1.6 points against an exact LRU simulated on the same requests, and in the model more samples recover none of it. Once each cached key is read no more often than every few seconds, the coarse clock stops mattering: at 5,000 a second, the model's LRU came within a quarter of a point of an exact LRU at every size.</p>
<p><strong>LFU's counters decay every minute.</strong> A benchmark run lasted about a minute, so almost nothing decayed. At 5,000 a second, many cached keys go several minutes between reads, their counters fall below the 5 a new key starts with, and LFU evicts them in favour of keys it has only just stored. Choose <code>lfu-decay-time 10</code> in the figure: in the model, with counters that last ten times longer, LFU leads at every size again.</p>
<p>The 5,000-a-second lines are a model, so I checked it on a real server with a scaled-down copy of the workload: 800,000 keys, 400 requests a second (about 5 to 6½ a minute per cached key), ten minutes to settle and ten measured. Redis agreed with the model to within 0.6 points in every case. <code>allkeys-lfu</code> hit 22.5% at this pace, against 27.5% when the same test ran as fast as possible (about 18,000 requests a second, roughly 240 a minute per cached key); with <code>lfu-decay-time 10</code> it hit 27.1%. <code>allkeys-lru</code> hit 17.7%, but with a smaller cache: on this build the same <code>maxmemory</code> held about 3,800 keys under LRU and 4,400 to 5,000 under the LFU runs. At equal size the model puts LRU about 3 points behind LFU at this pace, against 10 at full speed. (The default-decay LFU run once fell up to 48 seconds behind its schedule and then caught up; the model at its size agrees with it to 0.2 points.)</p>
<p>These requests are independent draws from a popularity ranking that never changes. That suits frequency counting, and suits a long decay most of all. Real traffic shifts: a product goes on sale, yesterday's favourite is forgotten, and a long <code>lfu-decay-time</code> keeps old favourites longer. Measure on your own traffic, at its real pace; the policy and <code>lfu-decay-time</code> can both be changed at runtime with <code>CONFIG SET</code>.</p>

<h3>Scans push out the useful keys</h3>
<p>There is one pattern where the difference between the policies is large. A nightly job exports every product, or a crawler walks every page, and each read goes through the cache. Each of those keys is read once and never again, but under LRU each arrives as the most recently used key in the cache. Database buffer pools have suffered from this for decades, which is why it has a name: <em>scan pollution</em>. Figure 9 feeds the same requests to two caches, one evicting by Redis's sampled LRU, one by its sampled LFU, and starts a batch job partway through.</p>
<ScanPollution number={9} />
<p>At the fast setting, the LRU cache's hit ratio drops by about a quarter while the job runs, because it gives room to keys nobody will read again. The LFU cache barely notices: every key the job adds starts at counter 5, below the popular keys, so the job's keys are the first to go. A real Redis at benchmark speed did the same, 49% falling to 37% under <code>allkeys-lru</code> and 53% to 51% under <code>allkeys-lfu</code>. Now set the pace to 1 request a minute per cached key. The LFU cache loses nearly as much as the LRU cache, because most of its counters have decayed below the job's 5. Set <code>lfu-decay-time</code> to 10 minutes and its protection returns.</p>
<p>So if batch jobs or crawlers read through your cache, keep them out of it: let them read the database directly, or skip storing on their misses. That works under any policy at any pace. <code>allkeys-lfu</code> protects only keys read more often than about once per <code>lfu-decay-time</code>, so if you rely on it, set the decay time from your traffic.</p>
</section>

<section id="observing">
<h2>Seeing expiry and eviction on a running server</h2>
<p>Redis counts both kinds of removal, and the counters answer the question you will be asked about any cache: why is the hit ratio lower than expected? These fields come from <code>INFO</code>.</p>
<div class="ee-table-wrap"><table class="ee-table">
<thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>What it tells you</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><code>expired_keys</code></td><td>Keys deleted because their TTL passed, by either mechanism.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>evicted_keys</code></td><td>Keys removed to stay under <code>maxmemory</code>.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>expired_stale_perc</code></td><td>The active cycle's running estimate of the share of keys with a TTL that have expired but are still in memory.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>expired_time_cap_reached_count</code></td><td>How often an expiry cycle ran out of its time budget and stopped. If it keeps rising, expiry is falling behind and clients are seeing its pauses.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>dbN:keys=…,expires=…</code> (<code>INFO keyspace</code>)</td><td>Keys, and how many of them have a TTL. Under a <code>volatile</code> policy, the difference is memory that can never be evicted.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>errorstat_OOM</code> (<code>INFO errorstats</code>)</td><td>Commands refused for lack of memory: under <code>noeviction</code> or a starved <code>volatile</code> policy, this should be zero.</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p>The Redis documentation suggests a simple diagnosis. If the hit ratio is lower than the working set suggests, look at <code>evicted_keys</code>: if it is high, the cache is too small for its working set or the policy is evicting the wrong keys. If evictions are low, look at <code>expired_keys</code>: if that is high, TTLs are shorter than the keys' useful life. <ArticleRef slug="operating-a-cache">Operating a cache</ArticleRef> turns these into alerts.</p>
</section>

<section id="decisions">
<h2>What you can now decide</h2>
<p>Choose a TTL from staleness first. Its cost, the share of hits that are stale, depends only on how fast the data changes; its benefit depends on how often each key is read, so popular keys need only short TTLs and rarely read keys cannot be rescued by long ones. Restate the TTL on every write, and jitter the TTLs of keys filled together. Expect expired keys to vanish from reads at once, but to linger in memory, key counts and notifications: about a tenth of the keys with a TTL on a busy server, a fifth or more for minutes after a burst or on a quiet one, and a single key for an hour or more on a large server.</p>
<p>Give a Redis used as a cache a <code>maxmemory</code> and an <code>allkeys</code> policy, and on a service whose default is <code>volatile-lru</code>, give every cache key a TTL. <code>allkeys-lru</code> is the safe default and, in the model, comes within a fraction of a point of an exact LRU once each cached key is read no more often than every few seconds; leave <code>maxmemory-samples</code> at 5. <code>allkeys-lfu</code> can do better when popularity is stable, but only with an <code>lfu-decay-time</code> long enough that the keys you want kept are read at least once per period. Judge either by your own traffic at its real pace, never by a fast benchmark. Keep batch jobs out of the cache, data that must not be evicted in a separate instance, and values small enough that one cannot empty the cache.</p>
<p>What a TTL cannot do is make a cached value correct the moment the database changes. That takes invalidation, and the races that come with it are the subject of <ArticleRef slug="invalidation">Cache invalidation and consistency</ArticleRef>. When many requests miss on the same popular key at the moment it expires, the problem is a stampede, the subject of <ArticleRef slug="stampedes">Cache stampedes and the thundering herd</ArticleRef>.</p>
</section>

